    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Heidlersburg was founded in 1812 by John Heidler because its crossroads location promised prosperity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        21,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hfdco25.com/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was once called Starry Town after Michael Starry built the first house and won a reward.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        10,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hfdco25.com/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Heidlersburg sits at the intersection of PA Route 234 and Old Harrisburg Road, the historic U.S. Route 30 corridor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hfdco25.com/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1863 the same roads that brought travelers also brought soldiers, linking Heidlersburg to the lead‑up to the Battle of Gettysburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hfdco25.com/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Chairmaking thrived in Heidlersburg during and after the Civil War, making the town a regional woodworking center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hfdco25.com/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Milton Pittenturf, a famed local carpenter, built at least six covered bridges in Adams County, many near Heidlersburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
